FIXED (mechanism qualified on silicon at a one-layer subset); full-shape 3x2 qualification IN FLIGHT

--- c-defects attempt 4, 2026-08-31 ---

Two models in one process could not both create a global circular buffer. Attempt 3
reduced the cause and implemented a shared placement record; this attempt got that onto
silicon and found the REST of the defect behind it.

WHAT ATTEMPT 3'S FIX DID, measured (logs/s1_qwen_two_pools_sub.log, 1-layer subset):
  the 21-minute hang in ttnn.synchronize_device is GONE. The second model is now stopped
  on the host, before anything is enqueued, in 106 s:
    RuntimeError: cannot restore the global circular buffer to its original L1 address:
    the lowest occupied L1 is 1272480, already below the free top 1305280 the first
    creation had
  That is a large improvement - a mesh-wedging hang became a diagnosable refusal - and it
  was not the gate.

WHAT WAS ACTUALLY WRONG, measured (logs/s3_qwen_two_pools_sub_table.log):
  The full resident L1 table on entry to BOTH creations, diffed:
    creation 1   lowest=1370816  102 blocks  128320 B  {32:94,1088,2048,5440,8192x2,17408x2,65536}
    creation 2   lowest=1272480  179 blocks  130784 B  {32:171,1088,2048,5440,8192x2,17408x2,65536}
  Every block larger than 32 bytes matches in size AND count. The entire difference is
  77 extra 32-byte blocks - 2464 B, 1.9%.
  So it is NOT a bigger configuration (doubling max_num_blocks 2048 -> 4096 changes L1 by
  nothing; the paged pool is not in L1), and NOT the 64 kB headroom leaking (there is
  exactly one 65536-byte block in EACH table - it did not leak, it MOVED, 109376 B down).
  D-C7's original 923776-of-1393472-B-per-bank leak is 99.7% gone.

  32 bytes is a semaphore. FreeListOpt::allocate takes the smallest free block that fits,
  so 77 holes are taken in preference to the low region and displace the 64 kB block from
  1381856 to 1272480 - below the recorded free top. The gate was lost to FRAGMENTATION by
  2464 bytes, not to capacity.

THE HOLDER: the ttnn program cache. It belongs to the mesh device, outlives close(), and a
cached program holds its semaphores as well as the buffer_address()/config_address() pair
captured once at circular_buffer.cpp:179 and re-sent by dispatch.cpp:3035 every launch.
One action fixes both halves.

FIX, committed faec6e59938:
  Prefetcher2DConfig.on_global_cb_released - a callable, default None (byte-for-byte the
  previous path), called once from cleanup() after the buffer's last reference is dropped.
  The module announces; it does not know what a program cache is or how many models share
  a mesh. release_galaxy_global_cb_placement in models/common/models/galaxy/prefetch.py
  decides: clear_program_cache, then forget the placement record.
  Host: test_prefetcher_2d.py 35 passed (33 before). Deleting only the two-line
  announcement in cleanup gives 1 failed / 34 passed.

ON SILICON (logs/s6_qwen_two_pools_sub_afterfix.log), the identical 1-layer case:
  1 passed in 52.68 s, and BOTH creations are in the log:
    model 1  [(513024, 192), (513216, 792064)]
    model 2  [(458272, 192), (458464, 792064)]
  lowest-occupied on entry to creation 2: 1272480 -> 1316064. The fragmenting blocks came
  back. The second model's buffer lands at a DIFFERENT address, which is correct: the pin
  exists only to stop a cached program reading a moved buffer, and there is no longer such
  a program. The guard still fires for the single-model release/recreate case.

STILL OWED: full 64-layer shape, three fresh processes, BOTH models (q11 t1-t6), and the
shared-code regression of the clash gate (q11 v1-v6). IN FLIGHT from 17:31Z.

COST TO NOTE: a model close now retires the mesh's program cache, so a process that closes
and rebuilds a model pays a full recompile. Correct, and worth c-perf-paired knowing.
